  <wpt lon="-111.03763818740845" lat="31.746124272078692">
    <name>Trailhead to the Juan Batista de Anza Trail </name>
    <cmt>In 1775-76 Juan Bautista de Anza, an expeditionary leader, military officer, and politician primarily in California and New Mexico under the Spanish Empire led some 240 men, women, and children on an epic journey to colonize the first non-Native settlement at San Francisco Bay. Today, the 1,200-mile Juan Bautista de Anza National Historic Trail connects history, culture, and outdoor recreation from Nogales, Arizona, to the San Francisco Bay Area. (https://www.nps.gov/juba/index.htm)</cmt>
    <desc>In 1775-76 Juan Bautista de Anza, an expeditionary leader, military officer, and politician primarily in California and New Mexico under the Spanish Empire led some 240 men, women, and children on an epic journey to colonize the first non-Native settlement at San Francisco Bay. Today, the 1,200-mile Juan Bautista de Anza National Historic Trail connects history, culture, and outdoor recreation from Nogales, Arizona, to the San Francisco Bay Area. (https://www.nps.gov/juba/index.htm)</desc>
