  <wpt lon="-110.88541137589324" lat="31.738501908830766">
    <name>Madera Canyon</name>
    <cmt>Just slightly off route is Madera Canyon, one of the most famous birding areas in the United States. It is a north-facing valley in the Santa Rita Mountains with riparian woodland along an intermittent stream, bordered by mesquite, juniper-oak woodlands, and pine forests. Madera Canyon is home to over 250 species of birds, including 15 hummingbird species. Visitors from all over the world search for avian specialties such as the Elegant Trogon, Elf Owl, Sulphur-bellied Flycatcher, Red-faced Warbler, and Painted Redstart. A comprehensive bird list published by FoMC is available at the Proctor Visitor Info Station and at trailheads. (https://friendsofmaderacanyon.org/)</cmt>
    <desc>Just slightly off route is Madera Canyon, one of the most famous birding areas in the United States. It is a north-facing valley in the Santa Rita Mountains with riparian woodland along an intermittent stream, bordered by mesquite, juniper-oak woodlands, and pine forests. Madera Canyon is home to over 250 species of birds, including 15 hummingbird species. Visitors from all over the world search for avian specialties such as the Elegant Trogon, Elf Owl, Sulphur-bellied Flycatcher, Red-faced Warbler, and Painted Redstart. A comprehensive bird list published by FoMC is available at the Proctor Visitor Info Station and at trailheads. (https://friendsofmaderacanyon.org/)</desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>Dot</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lon="-110.97345781020665" lat="31.687740340674793">
    <name>Forest Service Road 143 / Salero Canyon Road </name>
    <cmt>The surface and difficulty of Forest Road 143/Salero Canyon Road change year-to-year due to monsoons and the way the road is maintained. While the road offers some camping opportunities, incredible views of the Santa Cruz River Valley, and a vast diversity of fauna (a prickly pear cactus next to a cedar tree), this road has a reputation for crushing tired souls who are not mentally prepared for the persistent steep grades and loose surfaces. The rough and tough stuff lasts for approximately 12 miles. Plan accordingly! </cmt>
    <desc>The surface and difficulty of Forest Road 143/Salero Canyon Road change year-to-year due to monsoons and the way the road is maintained. While the road offers some camping opportunities, incredible views of the Santa Cruz River Valley, and a vast diversity of fauna (a prickly pear cactus next to a cedar tree), this road has a reputation for crushing tired souls who are not mentally prepared for the persistent steep grades and loose surfaces. The rough and tough stuff lasts for approximately 12 miles. Plan accordingly! </desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>Dot</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lon="-110.95036999077064" lat="31.674218554141326">
    <name>Fred Lawrence Whipple Observatory</name>
    <cmt>Visible from the route is the Fred Lawrance Whipple Observatory. The Fred Lawrence Whipple Observatory (FLWO) is the largest satellite facility of the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory (SAO), part of the Center for Astrophysics | Harvard &amp; Smithsonian. Located​ on Mt. Hopkins in southern Arizona​, this remote site is home to several telescopes, including the 6.5-meter MMT Observatory. The observatory also includes:
The Very Energetic Radiation Imaging Telescope Array System (VERITAS) gamma-ray observatory.
Telescope arrays designed to find Earth-like exoplanets.
Several other telescopes used for a wide range of astronomical observations.
The observatory is currently closed to the public; however, cyclists can enjoy a long dirt road climb with beautiful views to the 12 KM mark, before turning around. 
</cmt>
    <desc>Visible from the route is the Fred Lawrance Whipple Observatory. The Fred Lawrence Whipple Observatory (FLWO) is the largest satellite facility of the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory (SAO), part of the Center for Astrophysics | Harvard &amp; Smithsonian. Located​ on Mt. Hopkins in southern Arizona​, this remote site is home to several telescopes, including the 6.5-meter MMT Observatory. The observatory also includes:
The Very Energetic Radiation Imaging Telescope Array System (VERITAS) gamma-ray observatory.
Telescope arrays designed to find Earth-like exoplanets.
Several other telescopes used for a wide range of astronomical observations.
The observatory is currently closed to the public; however, cyclists can enjoy a long dirt road climb with beautiful views to the 12 KM mark, before turning around. 
</desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>Dot</type>
  </wpt>
